{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2821 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c62853e082d3d8af211e91418f01676fdd804f3873c642fe5ce63777836e2381" :edb/db-before-t 2820 :edb/db-after-t 2821 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536133 50 #inst "2026-10-09T16:10:51.167Z" 13194139536133 true] [17592186198318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385247 13194139536133 true] [17592186198318 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385254 13194139536133 true] [17592186198331 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385240 13194139536133 true] [17592186198331 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385261 13194139536133 true] [17592186198331 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385268 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385241 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1089 "q-161205" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/658f58654e8791f15fe6c2a9c719de3b02aa1c3912b4b6dc7b4e2fe2d2cdda62.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1113 #uuid "31ffa87a-5233-50d0-b7ee-8cee96dee4ef" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385240 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/05664db64ac6595ff0caec7daeec20cb3508d5c18a5266aa126295e3aa040233.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-3)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-0)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385242 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385243 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385244 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385245 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385246 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1041 "selection" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385243 13194139536133 true] [17592186385241 1110 #uuid "8ad142d9-66be-5329-95d4-f3b89982c4e9" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385242 1034 "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385242 1111 #uuid "7866db91-bf68-517e-a293-5ce51019b185" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385243 1034 "-2\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385243 1111 #uuid "bcf5a683-9999-53d1-9217-615ba13763f5" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385244 1034 "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385244 1111 #uuid "c3cd2220-5068-5836-be72-5e249ddb8867" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385245 1034 "\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385245 1111 #uuid "586c80c3-160c-535a-8b60-b513a90e6f90" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385246 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385246 1111 #uuid "9e84217d-a4fd-5797-821c-d3b3cd45c63c" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385248 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1089 "q-161235" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/c8c7f186ba8769231e9245e0887501f666e56c317fd4f546550202956365b5dc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1113 #uuid "99d508b2-0096-5ab9-9058-cfe5d27b4279" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385247 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x(x+yz)+y(y-xz)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+xyz+{y}^{2}-xyz+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385249 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385250 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385251 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385252 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385253 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1041 "selection" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385252 13194139536133 true] [17592186385248 1110 #uuid "95f2c4cf-7863-57ce-a193-fcaf40563c85" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385249 1034 "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385249 1111 #uuid "ed085b26-3972-5480-b2d4-9f135a827c4d" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385250 1034 "8\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385250 1111 #uuid "a51e482f-dff0-5017-b7a3-1ad65de3913a" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385251 1034 "16\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385251 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385251 1111 #uuid "98908a54-e1fa-5cd1-bac5-2726495b88ea" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385252 1034 "8\\pi \\sqrt{2}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385252 1111 #uuid "48801efc-38ef-569b-8136-d48f5b87a5a7" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385253 1034 "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385253 1111 #uuid "453d8cd0-b6f5-5243-9e5b-890dadb0cc0d" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385255 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1089 "q-161237" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/1ee9e32dc5dbe7ba1222a5448ce9ae1227a461a40a36f13e337fce7990263408.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $4$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1113 #uuid "0e7ff66f-9019-5ca1-9a08-6fd059549989" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385254 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(xz+0-xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385256 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385257 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385258 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385259 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385260 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1041 "selection" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385260 13194139536133 true] [17592186385255 1110 #uuid "e7d7b70b-64f6-556e-9638-6c47307b24ca" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385256 1034 "12\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385256 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385256 1111 #uuid "a20fa028-20b8-5ec4-948a-29f41b40ec05" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385257 1034 "4\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385257 1111 #uuid "6d15ea74-0302-5d54-9c43-2eb07001ff22" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385258 1034 "16\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385258 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385258 1111 #uuid "10194470-e73f-5a49-8715-ead856ccae47" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385259 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385259 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385259 1111 #uuid "56d4b35f-948d-57ec-828f-e6afa9398777" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385260 1034 "0" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385260 1111 #uuid "c0cb06a1-21dd-5ec5-83a0-621b57b3ed9c" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385262 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1089 "q-162543" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/7ca9996f6d0185d99752ccf5201b5300e0e5149ad674bb9676cf8c716b71a171.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1113 #uuid "d2c242af-4809-59f7-988e-e803e50d30f6" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385261 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/320e0c3333aa59f4e856d7d869d6fece09b178ac1abb3acd402621977df9b496.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2(3)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{3}{(25-{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\pi (64-125) \\\\  & =-4\\pi (-61) \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,2(0)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =244\\pi +0 \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385263 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385264 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385265 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385266 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385267 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1041 "selection" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385263 13194139536133 true] [17592186385262 1110 #uuid "8c5d6895-a6a1-5988-90fb-f47911c7c67a" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385263 1034 "244\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385263 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385263 1111 #uuid "64b4e220-0c2a-519b-b8f8-29bee0330094" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385264 1034 "81\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385264 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385264 1111 #uuid "65b48864-394b-569c-bdb8-58b6c495e8e8" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385265 1034 "64\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385265 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385265 1111 #uuid "6401b8d8-e574-54a2-80fd-118531576c7f" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385266 1034 "162\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385266 1111 #uuid "0d88b8c5-b21d-54f7-9b6e-e8823c275d1e" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385267 1034 "128\\pi " 13194139536133 true] [17592186385267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536133 true] [17592186385267 1111 #uuid "868fefd2-655c-5476-99c1-8be6bda7111a" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385269 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1089 "q-162547" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/dedd58b5eeeba8b2874c740543132026f95157597dd12ad31fbaa476a3d77c2e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1113 #uuid "4155410e-a945-5678-b663-42d0fd604e95" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385268 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/9ffcdd701a8ac6b12cd62773e1cabfd2ce95f9a809e18d087c4bf4f4a22eadb5.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,({4}^{2})(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{r}^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(6-{r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[3{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =32\\pi (\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,{0}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =216\\pi +0 \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536133 true] [17592186385269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385270 13194139536133 true] [17592186385269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385271 13194139536133 true] [17592186385269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385272 13194139536133 true] [17592186385269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385273 13194139536133 